  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x-3}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the right by $3$ units. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{x-3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251515/assets/e3308ad7f61359df7fb4e1b0f32f1d373c703be34b353ffa100ae57c6c115e0b.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x-3}=\\infty ,\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
